 "content": {
  "subject": "Measurements",
  "topic": "Extension of Range of PMMC Instruments",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Can a tiny milliamp meter safely measure a massive ten ampere line current?",
  "question": "A PMMC galvanometer has a coil resistance of 99 ohms and gives a full scale deflection for 10 milliamperes. What shunt resistance is required to extend its range to 10 amperes?",
  "options": [
   "0.099 ohms",
   "0.100 ohms",
   "0.990 ohms",
   "1.000 ohms"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 0,
  "answerLine": "The required shunt resistance is 0.099 ohms.",
  "explanation": [
   "To measure a larger current without burning the delicate coil, we place a low resistance in parallel.",
   "First, we calculate the multiplying power m, which is the total current divided by the meter current.",
   "The total current is 10 amperes, while the meter full scale deflection current is 10 milliamperes.",
   "Converting 10 amperes to milliamperes gives 10000 milliamperes, yielding a multiplying factor of exactly 1000."
  ],
  "funFact": "Shunts in high-precision ammeters are made of manganin because its resistance barely drifts with temperature changes.",
  "outro": "Remember, always divide by m minus one, not m! Follow for more electrical engineering prep!",
